erful book dancing in the streets  history of collective Joy by Barbara  aaronreich she says when the European  explorers went out in the you know 16th  17th century they found wherever they  went people danced around campfires had  rituals that bonded the group together  they often used psychedelic substances  they had beat Heavy Music so this is  human nature but feudal systems are also  pretty common there are a few major  patterns and so one possibility is  there's not just one way of doing things  but it's not anything goes it's that  there are several different sort of  grammatical human forms and this  actually became the basis of my major my  major work in Psychology which is moral  foundations Theory which is my effort to  say  what are the taste buds of the moral  sense because Cuisine varies around the  world you wouldn't say that the French  are wrong if they don't cook like the  Vietnamese but I think you could say  that the French and the Vietnamese are  both much better Cooks than the English  or at least you know they were  historical  it's not that anything goes there is  some truth  but here's where bringing the second  kind of abstract but really powerful  concept which is anthropocentric truth  so that means something can be true  relative to anthro to humans to who we  are so if if intelligent aliens come to  our solar system they will find that the  Earth is the third planet from the Sun  and that was true before we existed  it'll be true long after we're gone and  they would find that gold is a better  conductor of electricity than is  aluminum that was true before we were  here it'll be true you know so those are  non-anthropocentric truths there's a  fact and so many people have been on a  quest to find what are the moral truths  like and so Sam Harris who for a brief  time was my intellectual enemy we kind  of had like a feud for a few years now  we're friends we actually like each  other quite a lot but we had kind of a  feud over this because he has a whole  book of the landscape of morality  arguing that moral truths are like the  truths of chemistry and I say no  they are anthropocentric traces from a  philosopher David Wiggins  there are things that are true because  of the creatures we are so there are  things that are beautiful  because of our senses there are things  that Shakespeare is a better writer than  Kurt Vonnegut now I love reading Kurt  Vonnegut I probably enjoy it more than  Shakespeare but man I can see and almost  everyone can Shakespeare is a complete  genius at crafting words that's a fact  if aliens come here from another planet  they're not going to agree because it  doesn't make sense it wouldn't fit with  their nature anyway so putting this all  together I think what we can say is  there are moral truths and moral orders  that are emergent I am not a relativist  I am not a monist I'm a moral pluralist  there are multiple moral truths and  these truths are emergent from Human  Nature interacting as people interact in  societies okay wow I think I just did it  oh it's great

